      "text": "Given that when an integer \\( n \\) is divided by 15, the remainder is 7, we can express this mathematically as:\n\\[ n \\equiv 7 \\pmod{15} \\]\nThis means that \\( n \\) can be written in the form:\n\\[ n = 15k + 7 \\]\nfor some integer \\( k \\).\n\nNext, we need to find the remainders when \\( n \\) is divided by 3 and by 5. Let's start with the remainder when \\( n \\) is divided by 3.\n\nSince \\( n = 15k + 7 \\), we can express 15 as \\( 15 = 3 \\times 5 \\). Therefore, we can rewrite \\( n \\) as:\n\\[ n = 3 \\times 5k + 7 \\]\nWhen we divide \\( n \\) by 3, the term \\( 3 \\times 5k \\) is divisible by 3, so we only need to consider the remainder of 7 when divided by 3. We know that:\n\\[ 7 \\div 3 = 2 \\text{ remainder } 1 \\]\nTherefore:\n\\[ 7 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{3} \\]\nSo, the remainder when \\( n \\) is divided by 3 is 1.\n\nNext, let's find the remainder when \\( n \\) is divided by 5. We have already expressed \\( n \\) as:\n\\[ n = 15k + 7 \\]\nWe can see that \\( 15k \\) is divisible by 5, so we only need to consider the remainder of 7 when divided by 5. We know that:\n\\[ 7 \\div 5 = 1 \\text{ remainder } 2 \\]\nTherefore:\n\\[ 7 \\equiv 2 \\pmod{5} \\]\nSo, the remainder when \\( n \\) is divided by 5 is 2.\n\nFinally, we need to find the sum of these remainders:\n\\[ 1 + 2 = 3 \\]\n\nThus, the sum of the remainders when the same integer is divided by 3 and by 5 is:\n\\[ \\boxed{3} \\]",
